Z||000012||horseman||12-11-2000||07:43 PM||||Thsnkd Badlobo,<BR>I figured that they were all Comp. cam grinds. I will look up the Lunati cams at the websight. Is there that much difference between the two companies grinds? The Lunati appears to be more radical. If I choose the Lunati, then who should I buy the headers from? It seems like the logical way to go! Thanks again,<P>Charlie||||reg||14
Z||000013||88formula||12-11-2000||08:50 PM||||Hey horseman you cannot just put in a camshaft like the Lunati camshaft and expect a performance increase. You are likely to hurt performance than increase performance if you do that, even with headers. Everything in an engine is designed to work as a system. This means that you will have to modify the block, crankshaft, intake system, exhaust system, cylinder head, valve train, computer system, fuel system, and just about everything before you will ever see 192 horsepower from that camshaft and engine. <P>If fact I don't think that the 2.5-liter lower end would hold up under 6500 rpm and 192 horsepower for long before something breaks.<P>I think a camshaft like that should be used with the Super Duty block and crankshaft for reliability reasons.<P>You would be better off going with something less aggressive if you are planing on keeping much of the stock 2.5-liter engine.<P>The number one or two Comp camshafts would be a better choice because you would not have to do as many modifications to your engine, you would have more usable power and better derivability, and you would see an improvement in the rpm that you engine was designed to operate in.<P>I don't want you to be mislead by the dyno's I provided for baldlobo into believing that you will get 192 horsepower from your engine with just a camshaft change and headers. The Lunati camshaft is not just a bolt in modification because like I said before everything works as a system and the 2.5 engine is a low revving engine not designed for high rpm use. The Lunati camshaft is designed for high rpm engines with large intake runners and a high rpm capability. With the Lunati camshaft your engine would make less power than your stock camshaft does now in the rpm that your engine operates.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 12-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||horseman||12-11-2000||09:47 PM||||Thanks for the tips guys,<BR>regarding your advice; which of the top 3 Comp.Cams would provide the best compromise between mileage and performance? If I can't go with the radical Lunati then I might as well have a good daily driver eh.<BR>Thanks again,<BR>Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000015||88formula||12-12-2000||07:48 PM||||I would use the first camshaft.||||reg||1
